WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.140
Measles is one of the most contagious diseases
we know.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:09.600
Just one person is likely to infect between
12 and 18 people.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.700
That number is called a disease's "basic
reproduction number," or R-naught.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.520
Scientists use it to describe how contagious
a disease is.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.740
Zika? It has an R-naught of up to 6.6.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:23.420
Super contagious.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.580
But the seasonal flu? Just a little over one.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:29.880
And Covid-19, the disease caused by the coronavirus?

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.920
Just about two.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:36.460
That difference, between the flu and Covid-19,
doesn't seem so big.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:40.460
Especially when you look at them next to these
really contagious diseases.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:45.060
Plus, a lot of the symptoms of the flu and
Covid-19 are really similar:

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:48.460
A fever, cough, they can even both lead
to pneumonia,

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:51.220
which is ultimately how they can both be fatal.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:53.880
So, thinking of Covid-19 and the flu as similar

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:57.480
is a common, and frankly, understandable comparison to want to make.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:58.600
"This is like the flu."

00:00:58.610 --> 00:00:59.600
"It’s a lot like the flu."

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.980
"I like to believe that it's going to end up more
like the flu virus."

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:05.780
But wait, let’s go back to those R-naught
numbers again.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:12.920
If the flu has an R-naught of 1.3, that means
each person gets either one or two people sick.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:17.900
After ten rounds, that’s 56 total people.

00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:22.100
And if we run the model with Covid-19’s
R-naught at two, if one person with it

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.200
gets two people sick, who get two people
sick…

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.320
After ten rounds, that’s more than two thousand
people.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.780
This does not happen with the flu.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.799
Because Covid-19 is very different.

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:40.729
And understanding how is crucial to understanding
how dangerous it really is.

00:01:40.729 --> 00:01:43.460
And why we have to take it so seriously.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:53.820
When we start to look for that comparison
to the flu, we almost calm ourselves down.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:57.660
But unfortunately, that’s not quite the right
reaction here.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.899
Every year, the seasonal flu kills as many
as 60,000 Americans.

00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:06.869
As of late March, Covid-19 has killed about
34,000 — across the whole world.

00:02:06.869 --> 00:02:08.989
But scientists expect that number to go up.

00:02:08.989 --> 00:02:10.099
Way up.

00:02:10.099 --> 00:02:13.080
Tens of thousands of people a year die of
the flu.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.460
But we haven't had this for a year yet. We’ve only had this for a few months.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.300
The trouble is coming, and we can predict it,

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.700
and we can see what's coming, at least to a certain extent.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:29.160
The first really big difference between Covid-19
and the flu is how long it takes you to feel sick.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.440
From the moment you’re infected with Covid-19,

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.020
it usually takes five days before you start to feel symptoms.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:39.100
But it can take as long as 12 or even 14 days.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:42.780
This is the incubation period: the time
between when you catch it,

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.260
and when you first realize you’re sick.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:49.720
And scientists think you could be contagious
during most of this period.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:54.329
The flu, by comparison, has an average incubation
period of just two days.

00:02:54.329 --> 00:02:58.680
You get sick, and pretty soon you feel sick,
and you know you’re contagious.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.920
This is what we’re used to.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:03.689
But Covid-19 flips that around.

00:03:03.689 --> 00:03:08.140
You can be contagious and spreading it around
for several days, and up to two weeks,

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:10.819
before it even occurs to you that you're sick.

00:03:10.819 --> 00:03:13.819
And that leads to the next big difference.

00:03:13.819 --> 00:03:17.129
No human immune system had seen this virus
before.

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:20.800
Nobody has a natural immunity to it.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.995
In flu season, there’s always a number of
people in the population

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.260
who are already immune to the flu.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:30.280
That can be because they got their flu shot,
or maybe because they’ve already had that flu strain.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.760
And that limits the spread of the virus.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:37.500
When one person is contagious with the flu,
they can only spread it to people who aren’t immune.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.080
Who can only spread it to other people who
aren’t immune.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:43.800
That’s why scientists and doctors urge us
to get our flu shot every year:

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.319
If enough people have immunity, they can kind of shield
the virus from reaching others.

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:52.720
The more immunized people, the more they can
protect those that are susceptible to the virus.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.340
But Covid-19 is brand new.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:58.480
None of us have had it before, and there’s
no vaccine.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.980
Which means nearly everyone on the planet
is susceptible.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:05.460
So when a contagious person, who may not even
know they’re infected,

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:11.120
comes into contact with others, it can spread like wildfire.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:15.180
And then you see how much more dangerous Covid-19
can be.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:18.680
Only 2% of people with the flu need to be
hospitalized.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:22.780
But 20 to even 30% of people who test positive
for Covid-19 do.

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:27.250
And we’re still learning about Covid-19’s
fatality rate, but scientists think it’s

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:30.050
somewhere between 1 and 3%.

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:34.110
But the flu’s rate is even lower: 0.1%.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:38.199
And that’s a disease that can kill 60,000
people a year in the US alone.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:42.300
That’s what Dr. Anthony Fauci, the US’s
leading infectious disease expert,

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.100
tried to clear up to Congress.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:48.620
"People always say, well, the flu does this,
the flu does that.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:51.220
The flu has a mortality of 0.1%.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:54.270
This has a mortality of ten times that.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:58.120
This is a really serious problem that we have
to take seriously."

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:01.270
And he’s just talking about the average
across all age groups.

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:05.880
For older people, or people with compromised
immune systems, it can be way higher.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.220
And that’s especially scary when you remember
all the other things

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:11.020
that are different about Covid-19:

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:12.120
The infectiousness,

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:13.840
how hard it is to know that you have it,

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:15.750
how susceptible we all are to it.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:21.460
Estimates vary, but some scientists have warned
that between 20 and 60% of the world’s population

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:25.400
could become infected with the virus.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:32.260
Because Covid-19 has no cure and no vaccine,
the only defense we have against it is social.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:38.229
We can take ourselves out of the chain — not
with immunity, but with social distancing.

00:05:38.229 --> 00:05:43.110
Just physically not being around anyone, and
staying home as much as possible.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:46.750
But that only works if each of us takes it
seriously.

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:50.920
Which is why comparing it to the flu, while
understandable, is not helpful.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:56.960
It's natural to want to find a
comparison, to want to make this seem small.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:02.200
When we use the flu as the backdrop, it almost
numbs us, or it can numb us,

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.920
and make us maybe even feel better about the current situation.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:08.400
Because the current situation is bad.

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:10.340
So now is not the time to be numb.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:16.320
We need to be vigilant, and then use our vigilance
for useful actions.

